WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.580
Instructor: Next up we're gonna talk about git pull.

00:02.580 --> 00:06.030
So the git pull command is similar to fetch

00:06.030 --> 00:08.370
in that we use it to retrieve changes

00:08.370 --> 00:10.020
from some remote repo.

00:10.020 --> 00:11.130
But the big difference is

00:11.130 --> 00:14.343
that pull will actually update our HEAD branch.

00:15.349 --> 00:16.350
It updates our working directory.

00:16.350 --> 00:19.350
Whatever changes are retrieved or pulled down

00:19.350 --> 00:22.200
from GitHub or some other remote repo

00:22.200 --> 00:25.170
will be integrated into our local repo

00:25.170 --> 00:26.610
and our working directory.

00:26.610 --> 00:27.720
So think of it as,

00:27.720 --> 00:31.290
okay git, go and download the latest commits from GitHub

00:31.290 --> 00:33.600
and immediately update my working directory

00:33.600 --> 00:34.950
with those changes.

00:34.950 --> 00:39.090
You can kind of think of it as a git fetch plus a git merge.

00:39.090 --> 00:41.640
So fetch in the sense that we are updating

00:41.640 --> 00:43.080
the remote tracking branch

00:43.080 --> 00:45.510
with the latest changes from some remote.

00:45.510 --> 00:47.790
So go and get the latest changes from, you know,

00:47.790 --> 00:51.660
origin master or origin main or origin food.

00:51.660 --> 00:55.440
But then second step is take those changes

00:55.440 --> 00:56.970
from the remote tracking branch

00:56.970 --> 00:58.050
and merge them in

00:58.050 --> 00:59.880
or update my current branch

00:59.880 --> 01:02.043
that I'm on with those new changes.

01:03.000 --> 01:04.890
So the syntax looks like this,

01:04.890 --> 01:05.910
git pull,

01:05.910 --> 01:09.600
and then a remote name and a branch name.

01:09.600 --> 01:12.240
What really is important to understand is that where

01:12.240 --> 01:15.180
we run git pull from matters.

01:15.180 --> 01:17.130
So whatever branch I'm on,

01:17.130 --> 01:19.290
that is where the changes will be merged

01:19.290 --> 01:21.990
into where I'm pulling down to.

01:21.990 --> 01:23.490
So for example,

01:23.490 --> 01:26.250
if I am on the master branch

01:26.250 --> 01:29.700
and I run git pull origin master

01:29.700 --> 01:33.240
it's going to fetch the changes from origin master

01:33.240 --> 01:35.580
and then merge them into my current branch,

01:35.580 --> 01:38.820
which if I'm on master, then it's master.

01:38.820 --> 01:40.650
So it fetches and then merges.

01:40.650 --> 01:42.540
And just like any other merge,

01:42.540 --> 01:45.390
that can sometimes result in merge conflicts.

01:45.390 --> 01:48.600
So we need to resolve those just like a normal merge.

01:48.600 --> 01:49.890
So I'll demonstrate that,

01:49.890 --> 01:50.940
but why don't we start with something

01:50.940 --> 01:53.280
that won't result in a merge conflict?

01:53.280 --> 01:55.770
Alright, so let's use our movies branch.

01:55.770 --> 01:57.390
I think I'm, yeah, I'm already on it.

01:57.390 --> 01:58.830
We have this movies branch.

01:58.830 --> 02:01.110
I did fetch in the previous video.

02:01.110 --> 02:03.630
The most recent changes, right?

02:03.630 --> 02:07.410
We have Tinkerbell, but it's not on our actual branch.

02:07.410 --> 02:09.570
We just have it on the remote branch.

02:09.570 --> 02:11.910
So we have origin/movies

02:11.910 --> 02:14.350
but I don't have Tinkerbell here

02:15.210 --> 02:17.760
in my working directory on the movies branch.

02:17.760 --> 02:21.240
So if I want to get those changes from the movies branch

02:21.240 --> 02:26.240
I can do git pull origin and then the name of the branch

02:26.430 --> 02:29.100
I'm pulling movies and I wanna pull

02:29.100 --> 02:32.340
down to the branch I'm on, which is movies.

02:32.340 --> 02:34.920
So if you go to GitHub or this is gonna be hard

02:34.920 --> 02:38.310
for you to do unless you're editing the repo yourself

02:38.310 --> 02:39.330
and it'll have an exercise.

02:39.330 --> 02:40.163
So you get practice

02:40.163 --> 02:42.873
with this on the movies branch, we have Tinkerbell here.

02:43.920 --> 02:45.120
I don't have that commit here.

02:45.120 --> 02:49.830
So I'm gonna pull, and you can see it updates.

02:49.830 --> 02:52.920
If I type git log, I have a new commit here.

02:52.920 --> 02:56.490
So now my movies branch and origin movies

02:56.490 --> 02:59.250
they're pointing to the exact same commit, right?

02:59.250 --> 03:00.966
And if I type ls,

03:00.966 --> 03:04.470
we see Tinkerbell right there and over here I see that file.

03:04.470 --> 03:08.670
So I pulled that down and it was merged into this directory.

03:08.670 --> 03:12.480
So I'll do one more demo, I'll update the fantasy branch,

03:12.480 --> 03:16.320
I'll add another file just like this.

03:16.320 --> 03:20.160
It's a cyclops, I'll call it cyclops.txt.

03:20.160 --> 03:21.990
Again, this is on the fantasy branch.

03:21.990 --> 03:24.930
We're simulating somebody pushing up changes,

03:24.930 --> 03:26.340
some collaborator.

03:26.340 --> 03:28.350
So now we have that cyclops file.

03:28.350 --> 03:29.880
I don't have it locally.

03:29.880 --> 03:30.990
I'm on the movies branch,

03:30.990 --> 03:32.910
but I'm gonna switch to,

03:32.910 --> 03:33.743
what did we call it?

03:33.743 --> 03:34.623
Is it fantasy?

03:36.780 --> 03:39.060
So if I wanna get the cyclops file

03:39.060 --> 03:43.900
all I need to do is git pull and then origin fantasy.

03:46.680 --> 03:48.570
Okay, let's take a look.

03:48.570 --> 03:50.520
You can see it says create mode cyclops.

03:50.520 --> 03:53.823
That means it created my cyclops file.

03:54.822 --> 03:58.050
We see it here and over here we have access to it.

03:58.050 --> 04:02.040
Alright, so that's really the the core mechanic of pulling.

04:02.040 --> 04:03.721
What I'm gonna show you

04:03.721 --> 04:05.850
in the next video is that merge conflicts can arise

04:05.850 --> 04:08.310
and often do if you're working on something

04:08.310 --> 04:11.340
that somebody else also pushed some changes to.

04:11.340 --> 04:12.873
So that's coming up next.
